<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nerva–Antonine dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">96–<a href="/years/192">192</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Antonine dynasty, Five Good Emperors</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nerva–Antonine dynasty comprised seven Roman emperors who ruled from 96 to 192 CE: Nerva, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a>, Hadrian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antoninus-pius">Antoninus Pius</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-verus">Lucius Verus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commodus">Commodus</a>. The first five are popularly known as the Five Good Emperors, a term based on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Niccolò Machiavelli</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/discourses-on-livy">Discourses on Livy</a>. In five of its six successions the reigning emperor had no male heir and adopted the candidate of his choice, a bond that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a> made as strong as kinship. Marcus Aurelius's naming of his son Commodus as heir was considered an unfortunate choice, and the murder of Commodus in 192 ended the dynasty, followed by the Year of the Five Emperors.</p></div>
